# EOS Documentation

Apply when editing markdown, specs, ADRs, evidence notes, or Cursor rules.

## Canonical sources (cite, do not copy wholesale)

- `CONSTITUTION.md` / `docs/core/CONSTITUTION.md`
- `.agents/AGENTS.md`
- `docs/rules/CANONICAL_RULES_INDEX.json`
- `docs/specs/` and `docs/architecture/adrs/`
- `docs/evidence/`
- https://cursor.com/docs/rules — Project Rules must be `.mdc` with frontmatter

## Language and hierarchy

1. Constitution & governance
2. ADRs
3. Specifications
4. Implementation
5. Evidence

System artifacts: professional English. Do not paste the full constitution into a rule, PR, or skill. Link or cite article / rule ID (`R-EVIDENCE-01`, POL-001, …).

## Writing rules for agents

- Keep `.mdc` files under 500 lines. Frontmatter: `description` (string), `alwaysApply` (boolean), `globs` (comma-separated) only when `alwaysApply` is false and the rule is path-scoped.
- `alwaysApply: true` → omit `globs` (official docs: globs are ignored).
- Plain `.md` under `.cursor/rules/` is not a Project Rule.
- AI documentation is guidance, not enforcement.

## Specs and evidence

- OpenSpec filenames: `proposal.md`, `spec.md`, `design.md`, `tasks.md`.
- Record verification in `docs/evidence/` when the mission requires a sealed claim. Mark unverified text `NOT VERIFIED`.
- Estimate ≠ quote. Pricing needs a legal disclaimer.
- Do not claim `PRODUCTION_READY` in docs without executable evidence and PO sign-off.

## Preserve existing overlays

Leave `.cursorrules`, `eos-orchestrator.mdc`, and `engram.mdc` in place unless they are empty stubs of a numbered EOS rule file.
